---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Tools"
    categorySlug="tools"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Turn Your Knowledge Into Income with Online Courses</h2>
  <p>
    Creating and selling online courses is one of the best ways for remote workers, freelancers, and creators to build
    passive income. You create the content once and sell it indefinitely—no trading hours for dollars.
  </p>
  <p>
    But choosing the right platform matters. Some take massive cuts of your revenue. Others nickel-and-dime you with
    transaction fees. And some are so complex you'll spend more time fighting the software than creating content.
  </p>
  <p>
    We tested 10 course platforms over three months, creating actual courses and processing real transactions. We
    evaluated ease of use, pricing structures, marketing features, student experience, and overall value for creators.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Quick answer:</strong> <a href="#teachable">Teachable</a> is our top pick for most creators—it's the easiest
    to use with a solid free tier and reasonable pricing. <a href="#kajabi">Kajabi</a> is best if you want email
    marketing, sales funnels, and website hosting all in one platform. <a href="#thinkific">Thinkific</a> wins for
    customization with zero transaction fees.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">Our Top 3 Course Platform Picks</h2>
  <p>Short on time? Here are our top recommendations for course creators:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 online course platforms for creators"
  />
  <p>
    All three offer free trials or free tiers to test before committing. Keep reading for detailed reviews of all 8
    platforms we recommend.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Sell Courses Online -->
  <h2 id="why-sell-courses">Why Selling Online Courses Makes Sense</h2>
  <p>
    Online courses are one of the most scalable income streams for knowledge workers. Here's why creators are
    flocking to course creation:
  </p>

  <h3>Scalable Passive Income</h3>
  <p>
    Unlike consulting or freelancing where you trade time for money, courses scale infinitely. Create once, sell
    forever. Your 100th student costs you nothing extra to serve, but adds pure profit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Top course creators earn six and seven figures annually from courses they created years ago. Even modest success—a
    $200 course selling 10 copies per month—generates $24,000/year in largely passive income.
  </p>

  <h3>Establish Authority</h3>
  <p>
    Publishing a course positions you as an expert in your field. It's a credential that opens doors to speaking
    engagements, consulting opportunities, and higher-paying clients.
  </p>
  <p>
    Courses also build trust with your audience in ways that free content can't. Someone who invests $200 in your
    course pays more attention than someone reading a free blog post.
  </p>

  <h3>Help People at Scale</h3>
  <p>
    If you've ever wished you could help more people, courses let you do exactly that. Your knowledge reaches students
    across time zones while you sleep. You're no longer limited by your available hours.
  </p>

  <h3>The Market Is Massive</h3>
  <p>
    The global e-learning market exceeds <strong>$300 billion</strong> and grows 10-15% annually. Remote work has
    accelerated demand for online skills training. People are actively looking for courses in nearly every niche.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Reviews -->
  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">Course Platform Detailed Reviews</h2>

  <!-- Teachable -->
  <h3 id="teachable">1. Teachable — Best for First-Time Course Creators</h3>
  <p>
    Teachable pioneered the modern course platform and remains the easiest way to get your first course online. The
    intuitive interface means you'll spend time creating content, not wrestling with software.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Teachable stand out:</strong> The course builder is genuinely beginner-friendly. Drag and drop
    lessons, upload videos, and your course is ready to sell. Built-in payment processing means no merchant account
    headaches—Teachable handles everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Intuitive course builder:</strong> Drag-and-drop simplicity for creating courses</li>
    <li><strong>Built-in payments:</strong> Accept credit cards and PayPal instantly</li>
    <li><strong>Student management:</strong> Track progress, completion rates, and engagement</li>
    <li><strong>Coaching products:</strong> Sell 1:1 or group coaching alongside courses</li>
    <li><strong>Sales pages:</strong> Built-in page builder for marketing your courses</li>
    <li><strong>Affiliate program:</strong> Let others promote your courses for commission</li>
    <li><strong>Free tier:</strong> $1 + 10% per sale on free plan</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free plan ($1 + 10% transaction fee), Basic at $59/month (5% fee), Pro at $159/month (0%
    fee), Pro+ at $249/month. Annual billing saves 33%.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> We had a course live within two hours of signing up. The learning curve is
    minimal, and the built-in payment processing removed a major friction point.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> First-time course creators, solo entrepreneurs, and anyone who wants the simplest path
    from idea to selling. Great for validating course ideas quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Transaction fees on lower tiers add up quickly at scale. Customization options are
    limited compared to Thinkific. No built-in email marketing—you'll need ConvertKit or similar.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/teachable"
      text="Start Free with Teachable — Easiest Course Platform"
      variant="primary"
      program="teachable"
  />

  <!-- Thinkific -->
  <h3 id="thinkific">2. Thinkific — Best for Course Customization</h3>
  <p>
    Thinkific gives creators more control over their course experience and keeps more money in your pocket with zero
    transaction fees on all plans. If you want flexibility without paying Kajabi prices, Thinkific delivers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Thinkific stand out:</strong> No transaction fees means you keep 100% of your sales (minus
    payment processor fees). The course builder offers more customization than Teachable, including better quiz and
    assignment features.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero transaction fees:</strong> Keep all your revenue on every plan</li>
    <li><strong>Advanced course builder:</strong> Quizzes, assignments, certificates, and prerequisites</li>
    <li><strong>Site builder:</strong> Create a complete course website, not just course pages</li>
    <li><strong>Community features:</strong> Built-in community spaces for students</li>
    <li><strong>App store:</strong> Extend functionality with third-party integrations</li>
    <li><strong>White labeling:</strong> Remove Thinkific branding on higher plans</li>
    <li><strong>Free tier:</strong> 1 course, unlimited students, 0% transaction fee</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free plan (1 course), Basic at $49/month, Start at $99/month, Grow at $199/month. Annual
    billing offers significant savings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Thinkific's free plan is surprisingly generous—one full course with unlimited
    students. The learning curve is slightly steeper than Teachable but worth it for the flexibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Creators who want zero transaction fees, more customization options, or stronger
    quiz/assessment features. Good for course-based businesses at scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Interface is less intuitive than Teachable initially. No native email marketing—
    requires integration. Advanced features require higher-tier plans.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/thinkific"
      text="Try Thinkific Free — Zero Transaction Fees"
      variant="primary"
      program="thinkific"
  />

  <!-- Kajabi -->
  <h3 id="kajabi">3. Kajabi — Best All-in-One Platform</h3>
  <p>
    Kajabi is the premium choice for creators who want everything in one place: courses, email marketing, sales funnels,
    website, and community. The price is steep, but it replaces multiple tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Kajabi stand out:</strong> You genuinely don't need any other tools. Email sequences, landing
    pages, checkout, affiliate program, podcast hosting—it's all built in. For creators tired of stitching together
    multiple services, Kajabi simplifies everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>All-in-one platform:</strong> Courses, email, website, funnels in one tool</li>
    <li><strong>Email marketing:</strong> Built-in email with automation and sequences</li>
    <li><strong>Sales funnels:</strong> Pre-built pipeline templates for conversions</li>
    <li><strong>Community:</strong> Native community features for student engagement</li>
    <li><strong>Podcast hosting:</strong> Host and distribute podcasts directly</li>
    <li><strong>Zero transaction fees:</strong> Keep all your revenue</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile app:</strong> Branded mobile app for your students</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Basic at $149/month (3 products, 10K contacts), Growth at $199/month (15 products, 25K
    contacts), Pro at $399/month (100 products, 100K contacts). 14-day free trial.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Kajabi's value becomes clear when you calculate what you'd spend on separate tools
    for email, funnels, and course hosting. For serious course businesses, it often saves money.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Established creators building a full business, coaches with multiple offers, and anyone
    who values simplicity over cost optimization.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Expensive for beginners—$149/month is steep before you've validated your course.
    Some features are less powerful than specialized tools. Limited customization compared to Thinkific.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/kajabi"
      text="Try Kajabi Free for 14 Days — All-in-One Platform"
      variant="primary"
      program="kajabi"
  />

  <!-- Podia -->
  <h3 id="podia">4. Podia — Best for Digital Products + Courses</h3>
  <p>
    Podia positions itself as the platform for selling anything digital: courses, downloads, memberships, coaching, and
    webinars. If you sell multiple product types, Podia keeps everything organized in one place.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Podia stand out:</strong> The simplicity is refreshing. No transaction fees, unlimited
    everything, and a clean interface. Podia also includes email marketing, eliminating one more tool from your stack.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Multiple product types:</strong> Courses, downloads, memberships, coaching, webinars</li>
    <li><strong>Zero transaction fees:</strong> Keep 100% of your sales</li>
    <li><strong>Built-in email:</strong> Email marketing and automation included</li>
    <li><strong>Unlimited everything:</strong> No caps on products, customers, or emails</li>
    <li><strong>Affiliate marketing:</strong> Built-in affiliate program</li>
    <li><strong>Website builder:</strong> Create your entire site in Podia</li>
    <li><strong>Live chat:</strong> Built-in messaging with customers</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Mover at $39/month (email included), Shaker at $89/month (affiliates, advanced features).
    14-day free trial. No free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Podia is wonderfully simple. If you sell a mix of courses, downloads, and
    memberships, having everything in one dashboard is genuinely valuable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Creators selling multiple digital product types, those who want built-in email marketing,
    and anyone who values simplicity over advanced features.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Course features are less robust than Teachable or Thinkific. No free tier means
    upfront commitment. Limited quiz and assessment options.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/podia"
      text="Try Podia Free for 14 Days — Sell Anything Digital"
      variant="secondary"
      program="podia"
  />

  <!-- Skillshare -->
  <h3 id="skillshare">5. Skillshare — Best for Creative Skills</h3>
  <p>
    Skillshare is a marketplace platform where students pay a subscription to access all courses. You earn based on
    minutes watched rather than course sales—a fundamentally different model.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Skillshare stand out:</strong> Built-in audience of millions of students means you don't need your
    own marketing. Skillshare promotes your courses to interested learners. Great for creative skills where the platform
    audience aligns well.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Built-in audience:</strong> Millions of active subscribers</li>
    <li><strong>No marketing required:</strong> Skillshare promotes courses internally</li>
    <li><strong>Royalty model:</strong> Earn based on premium minutes watched</li>
    <li><strong>Free to publish:</strong> No upfront costs to create courses</li>
    <li><strong>Creative community:</strong> Strong in design, illustration, photography</li>
    <li><strong>Project-based learning:</strong> Students complete projects for engagement</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free to publish. Teachers earn royalties from a pool based on minutes watched. Typical
    earnings range from $0.05-0.10 per minute watched.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Skillshare works well for building initial audience and exposure, but earnings per
    student are lower than selling your own courses. Best as a supplement, not primary platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Creative skills teachers, those without existing audiences, and creators who want passive
    discovery without marketing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Lower earnings per student than direct sales. You don't own the customer
    relationship. Course length affects earnings significantly.
  </p>
  <p class="text-white/80 italic">
    Note: Skillshare doesn't offer an affiliate program. We include it because it's a legitimate option for creators.
  </p>

  <!-- Udemy -->
  <h3 id="udemy">6. Udemy — Best for Built-In Audience</h3>
  <p>
    Udemy is the largest course marketplace with 57 million students. Publishing on Udemy gives you instant access to
    massive discovery potential—but at the cost of significant revenue share.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Udemy stand out:</strong> The marketplace effect is real. Students searching for topics discover
    your course without you doing any marketing. For new creators without audiences, this discovery is valuable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>57 million students:</strong> Massive built-in marketplace</li>
    <li><strong>SEO and discovery:</strong> Courses rank in Google and Udemy search</li>
    <li><strong>Free to publish:</strong> No platform fees upfront</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews and ratings:</strong> Social proof drives more sales</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile apps:</strong> Students access courses on iOS and Android</li>
    <li><strong>Q&A features:</strong> Built-in student support tools</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free to publish. Revenue share: 37% for Udemy-generated sales, 97% for instructor-
    generated sales (with coupon). Frequent sales pressure pricing down.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Udemy is excellent for discovery but frustrating for pricing. Constant sales mean
    your $200 course often sells for $12.99. Best combined with your own platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> New creators without audiences, supplemental income alongside your own platform, and
    topics with high search volume.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Heavy revenue share (up to 63%). Constant discounting devalues courses. You don't
    own customer relationships. Pricing pressure is significant.
  </p>
  <p class="text-white/80 italic">
    Note: We include Udemy as a legitimate option, though we don't earn commissions from recommending it.
  </p>

  <!-- Gumroad -->
  <h3 id="gumroad">7. Gumroad — Best for Simple Digital Sales</h3>
  <p>
    Gumroad is the simplest way to sell anything digital—courses, ebooks, templates, software. If you want minimal
    friction between your content and customers' credit cards, Gumroad delivers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes Gumroad stand out:</strong> You can literally have something for sale in 10 minutes. No complex
    setup, no learning curve—just upload, set a price, and share your link. The 10% fee is simple to understand.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Extreme simplicity:</strong> Sell anything in minutes</li>
    <li><strong>Flat fee structure:</strong> 10% of sales, no monthly fees</li>
    <li><strong>Email marketing:</strong> Basic email features included</li>
    <li><strong>Membership support:</strong> Sell subscriptions and memberships</li>
    <li><strong>Audience building:</strong> Gumroad discover page for exposure</li>
    <li><strong>Flexible pricing:</strong> Fixed price, pay-what-you-want, or free</li>
    <li><strong>Unlimited products:</strong> No caps on what you sell</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free to use. Gumroad takes 10% of each sale plus payment processing (approximately 2.9% +
    $0.30).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> Gumroad is perfect for quick launches and testing ideas. The 10% fee is higher than
    dedicated platforms at scale, but the simplicity is worth it for smaller creators.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Quick course launches, testing course ideas, creators who sell multiple digital product
    types, and anyone who values simplicity above all else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Course features are basic—no quizzes, certificates, or advanced student tracking.
    10% adds up at scale. Limited customization options.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/gumroad"
      text="Start Selling on Gumroad — No Monthly Fees"
      variant="ghost"
      program="gumroad"
  />

  <!-- LearnWorlds -->
  <h3 id="learnworlds">8. LearnWorlds — Best for Interactive Courses</h3>
  <p>
    LearnWorlds focuses on creating engaging, interactive learning experiences. If your courses need sophisticated
    assessments, interactive video, or compliance certifications, LearnWorlds has the tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What makes LearnWorlds stand out:</strong> Interactive video features let you add quizzes, buttons, and
    branching directly inside videos. The assessment engine supports complex exam scenarios for professional training.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key features:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interactive video:</strong> Add clickable elements and quizzes within videos</li>
    <li><strong>Advanced assessments:</strong> Question banks, time limits, randomization</li>
    <li><strong>SCORM support:</strong> Import existing e-learning content</li>
    <li><strong>Certificates:</strong> Customizable completion certificates</li>
    <li><strong>White labeling:</strong> Fully branded experience</li>
    <li><strong>Mobile app:</strong> Branded iOS and Android apps available</li>
    <li><strong>Community:</strong> Built-in social learning features</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Starter at $29/month ($5 per sale), Pro Trainer at $99/month (0% fee), Learning Center at
    $299/month (advanced features). 30-day free trial.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Our experience:</strong> LearnWorlds shines for professional training and compliance courses. The interactive
    video features are genuinely differentiated—we haven't seen them executed this well elsewhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Professional training providers, corporate learning, compliance courses, and anyone who
    needs sophisticated assessment features.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Minor drawbacks:</strong> Starter plan charges per-sale fees. More complex than Teachable—steeper learning
    curve. Overkill for simple courses.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/learnworlds"
      text="Try LearnWorlds — 30-Day Free Trial"
      variant="ghost"
      program="learnworlds"
  />

  <!-- How to Choose -->
  <h2 id="how-to-choose">How to Choose the Right Course Platform</h2>
  <p>
    With so many solid options, here's how to pick the right platform for your specific situation:
  </p>

  <h3>By Experience Level</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Complete beginner:</strong> Teachable (easiest setup)</li>
    <li><strong>Some tech comfort:</strong> Thinkific (more features)</li>
    <li><strong>Established creator:</strong> Kajabi (all-in-one)</li>
    <li><strong>Just testing an idea:</strong> Gumroad (fastest launch)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>By Business Model</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Single course focus:</strong> Teachable or Thinkific</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple products:</strong> Podia or Kajabi</li>
    <li><strong>Coaching + courses:</strong> Kajabi or Teachable</li>
    <li><strong>Corporate training:</strong> LearnWorlds or Thinkific Plus</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>By Budget</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free to start:</strong> Gumroad, Thinkific Free, or Teachable Free</li>
    <li><strong>Under $50/month:</strong> Podia ($39), Thinkific Basic ($49)</li>
    <li><strong>Under $100/month:</strong> Teachable Pro, Thinkific Start</li>
    <li><strong>Premium all-in-one:</strong> Kajabi ($149+)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Our Recommendation</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Start with free tiers:</strong> Test Teachable or Thinkific free to validate your course idea</li>
    <li><strong>Upgrade when needed:</strong> Move to paid plans when transaction fees exceed subscription cost</li>
    <li><strong>Consider all-in-one later:</strong> Kajabi makes sense once you're earning consistently</li>
    <li><strong>Don't overthink it:</strong> The platform matters less than creating and marketing your course</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Getting Started -->
  <h2 id="getting-started">Getting Started with Your First Course</h2>
  <p>
    Ready to create your course? Here's a practical roadmap:
  </p>

  <h3>Step 1: Validate Your Idea</h3>
  <p>
    Before building a full course, confirm people will pay for it. Pre-sell to your audience, create a mini-course, or
    survey potential students. Validation prevents wasted effort on courses nobody wants.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 2: Outline Your Curriculum</h3>
  <p>
    Map out the transformation students will experience. Start with the end result, then work backward to identify the
    steps needed to get there. Break those steps into modules and lessons.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 3: Create Your Content</h3>
  <p>
    Start recording. Don't aim for perfection—aim for done. Your first course won't be your best, and that's fine.
    Students care about results, not production quality.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 4: Set Up Your Platform</h3>
  <p>
    Upload your content to your chosen platform, create your sales page, and configure checkout. Most platforms make
    this straightforward—you can be live within a day.
  </p>

  <h3>Step 5: Launch and Iterate</h3>
  <p>
    Launch to your audience, gather feedback, and improve. Your course gets better with each cohort of students. Focus
    on helping students succeed and the rest follows.
  </p>

